ROLE: Act as a code agent. Make one correction to the uncommitted change in `.skilled/hooks/dispatch/pi/dispatch-preflight-lint.ts` and its test.

Problem: the `effectiveKind` helper in the `tool_call` handler turns an `ambiguous` inspection with one resolved packet into `direct`. That was not asked for and loosens the gate: `devin -p "$TASK"` and `bash -c "cd \"$DIR\" && devin -p task"` are ambiguous on purpose and must stay denied. Only a `none` inspection may be upgraded.

Fix:
1. In `effectiveKind`, delete the `inspection.kind === 'ambiguous'` branch so an ambiguous inspection stays `ambiguous`. Keep the `none` rules and the `direct` rule as they are. Update the comment above the helper so it no longer says a wrapper word reads as ambiguous and is resolved; say that a wrapper (`command`, `nohup`, `perl -e ... --`) in front of `codex exec` or `opencode run`, or a heredoc a shell runs, reads as none, and that an ambiguous reading is never upgraded.
2. In `.skilled/hooks/dispatch/pi/dispatch-preflight-lint.test.ts`, in the tests you added:
   - 'allows a named dispatch behind an exec wrapper': use raw text `dispatch via cli-opencode` and command `nohup opencode run -m p/m "task" </dev/null`.
   - 'blocks a named wrapper dispatch that omits the stdin redirect': use raw text `dispatch via cli-opencode` and command `nohup opencode run -m p/m "task"`.
   - add one `it('keeps an ambiguous dispatch denied even when it is named', ...)`: raw text `dispatch via cli-devin`, command `devin -p "$TASK" </dev/null`, expect `result?.block` to be `true`.
   If `opencode` must be on the stubbed PATH for these, add it to the existing stub list.
Keep every other test unchanged.

Comments keep the durable WHY and never name spec paths, phases, or task, review or finding ids.

